      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\), we need to determine \\(3^{12000} \\mod 1000\\). We are given that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), which means that the order of 3 modulo 1000 is 400. This means that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), and the exponent 12000 can be reduced modulo 400. Specifically, we need to find \\(12000 \\mod 400\\):\n\n\\[\n12000 \\div 400 = 30 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\implies 12000 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{400}\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that \\(3^{12000} \\equiv 3^{0} \\pmod{1000}\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\n3^{12000} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\nSo, the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\) are \\(\\boxed{001}\\).",
